<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sulla's civil war</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/83-bce">83</a>–82 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Sulla's second civil war · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>'s civil war was fought in 83–82 BCE between Lucius Cornelius Sulla, returning from the war against Mithridates, and the Marian faction governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Sulla landed at Brundisium, and with commanders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> defeated the Marian and Samnite armies, finally at the Colline Gate outside Rome in November 82 BCE. He then became dictator and carried out bloody proscriptions.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After his first march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to fight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mithridatic-war">First Mithridatic War</a>. In his absence <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a> and Lucius Cornelius Cinna took back the city from Sulla's supporter Gnaeus Octavius; Marius's soldiers killed leading Sullans, some 100 nobles in all, Sulla was declared an exile and his laws annulled. Marius died soon after his election as consul for 86 BCE, leaving Cinna in control. Cinna was later killed in a mutiny at Ancona, and with Mithridates defeated for the moment, Sulla turned back to Italy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the spring of 83 BCE Sulla landed in two divisions, at Brundisium and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a>. Outlawed nobles joined him, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-pius">Quintus Caecilius Metellus Pius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Marcus Licinius Crassus</a> with armies of their own, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> raised three legions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picenum">Picenum</a>. Sulla routed the consul Gaius Norbanus at the Battle of Mount Tifata, and the army of the other consul, Scipio Asiaticus, went over to him during negotiations at Capua.</p><p>In 82 BCE Sulla defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius-the-younger">Gaius Marius the Younger</a> at Sacriportus, and Marius retreated to Praeneste, where he was besieged. Before Sulla reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> the praetor Lucius Junius Brutus Damasippus had Sullan sympathisers killed, among them the Pontifex Maximus Quintus Mucius Scaevola. In the north Metellus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> wore down Gnaeus Papirius Carbo, who finally fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. A Samnite and Lucanian army under Pontius Telesinus and Marcus Lamponius then marched on Rome, and at the Colline Gate about 50,000 men are estimated to have died. Some 8,000 prisoners were executed afterwards on Sulla's orders, and Marius took his own life at Praeneste.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> went on to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, where he had Carbo sentenced to death, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Quintus Sertorius</a> was driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> only to return the next year. The proscriptions killed some 1,500 senators and equites and perhaps as many as 9,000 people, many for their property, and the sons and grandsons of the proscribed were barred from office for over 30 years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> stripped the tribunes of most of their powers, doubled the Senate from 300 to 600 members and gave it control of the courts. Near the end of 81 BCE he resigned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> and was elected consul for 80 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sulla's civil war” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
